<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olga of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 890–<a href="/years/969">969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Olga · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olga was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> for her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> after Igor's death in 945. She took harsh revenge on the Drevlians for her husband's killing and reorganised the collection of tribute. Baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 950s, she was the first ruler of Rus' to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olga-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Olga of Kiev" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikhail Nesterov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Olga_by_Nesterov_in_1892_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> gives Olga's origin as Varangian and her birthplace as Pleskov. Little is known of her life before her marriage to Prince Igor I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, the son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a>, and the birth of their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a>. According to the historian Alexey Karpov she was no more than 15 years old when she married.</p><p>In 945 Igor went to the Drevlian capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korosten">Iskorosten</a>, to force the Drevlians to pay tribute. They paid, but on the way home he decided the sum was too small and returned with only a small escort. The Drevlians murdered him. The Byzantine chronicler Leo the Deacon says he was tied to tree trunks and torn in two, a detail that the historian D. Sullivan suggests Leo may have invented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Olga took power as regent for her three-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> and was the first woman to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> tells how she answered a Drevlian proposal that she marry their Prince Mal. She had one embassy of twenty men buried alive in their boat, burned a second party of leading men in a bathhouse, and had Drevlians killed at a funeral feast beside Igor's tomb, five thousand according to the chronicle. After a year-long siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korosten">Iskorosten</a> she asked for three pigeons and three sparrows from each house and set the city alight by tying burning sulphur to the birds. The historian Boris Akunin finds only the killing of the first envoy plausible.</p><p>She changed the collection of tribute and set up trading posts, hunting grounds and boundary posts, among them the administrative and trading centres called pogosti. In the 950s she travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to visit Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>. The sources disagree about the visit. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-ceremoniis">Book of Ceremonies</a> mentions no baptism, John Skylitzes says she was baptised there, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> adds a marriage proposal that she refused, since baptism made the emperor her godfather. Church Slavonic sources give 957 for her baptism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She sent an embassy to the Holy Roman Emperor Otto I in 959, asking for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and priests, but Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> was expelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a>'s pagan allies. Sviatoslav refused to be baptised, though he did not hinder those who were. According to the Primary Chronicle Olga died of illness in 969 and was buried by her priest Gregory without a pagan funeral feast.</p><p>Her grandson Vladimir adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 988. Olga is venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> with the epithet Equal to the Apostles, her feast day being 11 July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> officially named her a saint in 1547, though John Fennell argued that she was most likely canonised in 1284.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olga of Kiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oleg and his successors Igor, <b>Olga</b> and Sviatoslav I extended tribute over East Slavic tribes such as the Polianians, Drevlians, Severians and Krivichians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Envoys of the pagan ruler of the Kievan Rus’ reported that inside it “we did not know where we were, in heaven or on earth”, and <b>Olga of Kiev</b> was probably baptised in its baptistery.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Igor's widow <b>Olga</b> took vengeance in 946, burying and burning Drevlian envoys and setting Iskorosten alight with birds carrying burning tinder.</blockquote><a href="/state/drevlians">Drevlians</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.82,28.33&amp;year=941" aria-label="Pskov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5156 209 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olga of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5606 479h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5606 479h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.82,28.33&amp;year=941">What was here in 941 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
